module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_resident_cost_taken_before_the_baseline_of_its_own_growth_check

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CeilingNotStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_resident_cost_taken_before_the_baseline_of_its_own_growth_check: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_resident_cost_taken_before_the_baseline_of_its_own_growth_check" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CeilingNotStated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a growth-bounded resident check reads its baseline at the first point it measures, so any cost the SAME process acquired before that point is folded into the baseline instead of being measured against it. In the heal regen round the one-resident-generation admission (v2.workflow.regen_convergence_transaction.regen_admit_generation_begin) compares orchestrator_resident_bytes_now against orchestrator_resident_bytes_at_first_generation plus a ceiling, and the round driver run_regen_round_cost (v1 seed, src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs) can acquire whole-tree resolve indices in the orchestrator process before generation 0 is admitted, when its affected_scope selection is requested through claim_executor --regen-affected-scope. That selection reaches regen_emission_scope_for_diff, which reaches regen_scope_worktree_admitted, render_scope_selection, and affected_set_bound_for (through regen_module_edges and render_affected_set_bound), each building the whole-tree index whose cost the same file measures elsewhere. None of it can trip the bound, because all of it lands in the floor the bound is added to.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE. Ask WHEN the resident state was acquired, not only what acquired it: before the first measurement of the check that is supposed to bound it, or after. A cost taken before the first measurement is unmeasurable by that check at any size, and the check still reports admitted. Where a process also runs generations, the question becomes whether the acquisition precedes the first generation's admission.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT: this is not 'the orchestrator holds too much', and it is not a breach of the one-resident-generation contract as declared. Pre-baseline round-driver work is not generation state, and moving it off the parent is not what this row asks. What it asks is that a resident ceiling name the population it bounds and the instant from which it measures. A ceiling whose baseline is taken inside the measured process bounds only growth-after-that-instant, which is a different guarantee from the one its name advertises.",

    "STRUCTURAL PREDICTION (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary, stated before the confirming run): a round run with the affected-scope selection requested must show its first-generation baseline raised by the scope selection's own index cost while the admission still reports admitted; the same round with the selection off must keep its baseline at the demand-route floor. PR #16012 records the off half only: every baseline measured so far sits at the floor, because no run has requested the selection. The raising half is prediction, not observation, until a run requests it.",

    "RUNG: unread, cause CeilingNotStated. No ceiling is stated anywhere for orchestrator-resident cost acquired before the first generation's admission, so there is no rung to read -- the guarantee the check's name advertises and the guarantee it delivers differ in the population they bound, not in how reliably they hold. The repair is not to raise a bound; it is for the ceiling to state the population it bounds, or for the acquisition to leave the measured process the way generation state already does.",

    "CONSUMPTION: this per-class record joins the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster. Production consumer: the heal regen round's generation-begin admission, v2.workflow.regen_convergence_transaction.regen_admit_generation_begin. WHAT WOULD MOVE IT OFF UNREAD: a round run with the affected-scope selection requested that reports a first-generation baseline including the scope selection's index cost, beside an admission that states which population its ceiling bounds at the place the bound is read, not in a comment.",
    "SIGHTING 2026-11-11 (srv1, unit red16251, real heal invocation on PR #16251 head ba126e56719cf973081b48ef9401ce94acbed90a, base mirror 84f93b33ea843fe1a843b6183b05882fc0314403, identity tree:sha1:120b520230d261e99de8f368abef0cf986cd7e57, log srv1:/tmp/red16251.log, no run cap): the affected-scope selection WAS requested (`claim_executor --regen-round-cost --base-mirror --regen-affected-scope --source-root dag --source-root src/v2`) and reported scope WholePopulation over a 302-path src/v1/stage0 population (paths=302, paths_rewritten=0, head_added_paths_kept=1), with round.seed_build, round.built_seed_regen (25 min), round.install (30 min, partition-move explanation inside it) and round.rebuild_from_installed (7 min) all driven in the orchestrator process. The bound refused the NEXT generation, not this one: `generation begin Refused: GenerationStateRetainedByOrchestrator ordinal=1 orchestrator_resident_bytes_at_first_generation=234332160 orchestrator_resident_bytes_now=5248946176 growth_bound_bytes=3221225472` -- growth of 5.0 GB against a 3 GiB bound, exit 1 after 1h42m. WHAT THIS READING BEARS ON: it does NOT confirm this row's baseline-raising prediction -- the first-generation baseline stayed at 234332160, the floor, even with the selection requested, so the pre-baseline index cost the row predicts is still unobserved. It IS the other open trigger on this row, whether the growth a bound admits is demanded by the next transition or retained from the last: here the retained half is the generation that just ran, its four emitted surfaces and its partition-move explanation (the explanation supervisor peaked near 20.8 GB of slot memory and its Explained result returns into the same process). REPEATED, NOT A ONE-OFF: the same refusal ended heal attempt 2 of PR #16220 in the same shape, so at this population size a run cannot reach a second generation at all, and any change that needs a second generation to prove its emission is stable meets this wall whatever it is otherwise doing. KEPT SEPARATE FROM THIS ROW'S CLAIM: the admission worked as declared on the population it bounds; what remains unowned is why the parent still holds 5 GB between generations, which is the deletion the row says would drop the bound.",

    "POINTER, 2026-11-11 (PR #16318; srv1 units jt2-16251 and jt4-16251, the same heal shape with the generation-begin phases in the image): the sighting above attributes the ~5 GB to the generation that just ran -- its emitted surfaces and its partition-move explanation. That attribution is measured FALSE and must not be carried forward. The generation-0 child peaks near 11 GB inside its OWN process and releases all of it at exit: the parent reads back at 233,512 kB on the line after parent.end generation=0. The explanation child peaks near 17 GB and falls back the same way. The whole growth is the orchestrator's own read AFTER generation 0 closes -- one settle of gunbc.regen_round_cost, modules=747 items=24156, VmRSS 233,700 kB to 5,124,580 kB, resident into the admission. And this is not this row's direction either: the cost is taken AFTER the baseline of its own growth check, which is why the bound saw it and refused it -- the contract working as written, not a cost hidden under the baseline. The receipt belongs to, and is on, gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_instrument_resolves_wider_than_the_subject_it_measures: the decider resolved a population to answer eleven questions about three integers. Scoping that resolve left the same runs inside this row's bound (ordinal=1 orchestrator_resident=3,002,527,744 over a 235,999,232 baseline against 3221225472), which is also the evidence that the bound was never the thing to change.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
